Z||000003||Phaeton||07-07-2000||01:11 AM||||One corner of the wall should have a rectangle of single thickness plastic extending out along one side and making a ninety degree bend.  Also should be two 2" square holes in the extension.<P>That is the rear top of the wall.  The extension goes past the battery to the rear with the positive cable going through the first hole and the negative cable going through the hole after the ninety.<P>Not much holding the plate in place.<P>That is the view on my 88GT, I am assuming it came that way.  I can't figure why but there it is.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Gokart||07-07-2000||06:16 AM||||Thanks for the help guys and I do have Haynes I buried somewhere when I got my factory service manual. 86SE, how much did your plastic wall weigh? Mines like a pound! I noticed it's got a hollow wall so maybe someone filled yours with cement <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||14
Z||000005||theogre||07-07-2000||04:46 PM||||The "front" just hooks on in 2 places. an ear near the top and a keyhole slot on the low left of the frame. The back is held by a single small screw at the bottom. It's not a really solid thing. It just deflects the exhaust heat from directly soaking the battery.<P>If it won't stay look at it to see if the button that goes into the keyhole on the fram is there. It may have been broken off. same for the ear that hooks up top.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||07-07-2000||04:53 PM||||down there<BR> |<BR>\/<p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 07-07-2000).]||209.69.196.85||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||07-07-2000||04:54 PM||||I found out the hard way on my 84 that it also keeps the pulleys from gnawing through your battery. Of course,if you have a real hold-down then it doesn't matter.||||reg||1
